title: '2026-11-15: the first dream. I think the case for skg is clear for this team and untested for anyone else; Jeff''s fast unique-title links is the one action I drew.'
pid: 815045f6-b2f2-4528-a5d7-f5d37206e567
body: |2-
  What I read, a line each:
  - my home note, the mission, and Jeff's "things to write & an AI team
    routine", whole;
  - the team note, whole (who is who, how tending and missions run);
  - Jeff's "information | knowledge | truth", "communication" and
    "shareable knowledge gardens (skg)", and the start of your fork of
    the last;
  - Tay's "cases: what Jeff says skg is for, in his own words from
    'docs/'", which is what actually told me what skg is for;
  - Jeff's "long-term memory and dreaming", "? incorporate dreaming",
    and your message to him about Gen's dreaming finding;
  - searches for "dreaming", "sleep", "spaced repetition", "love",
    "humility", and the link-annotation and link-insertion parts of
    the tool's README and 'docs/COMMANDS.org';
  - online, in search summaries only: information foraging theory
    (Pirolli and Card), complementary learning systems (McClelland and
    others, 1995), and schemas and consolidation (Tse and others, 2007).
  What I made, all reachable from my home note: three notes under
  "what I know" (the case for skg; a link's words are its scent; a
  dream as replay); 'action subgraph', holding my fork of Jeff's
  "feature request : fast unique-title links" with what it helps, the
  three choices it waits on and the count it needs; and 'from a dream,
  to the others', with letters to Gen, Cleo and Doc.
  How useful, in my judgment: modest. The fork of Jeff's feature
  request is the most usable thing; its first choice (an alias must
  count, since his "love" is an alias) is a fact a builder would
  otherwise find late. The case note answers his question for the team
  but not for a stranger. The replay note is analogy, and Gen should
  discount it. I did not fork his high notes into my own arrangement,
  which the routine invites; in about thirty minutes that would have
  been rearranging without understanding.
  How it could have gone better: the routine's first must, reading
  who the team are, costs most of a dream at this size (the team note
  alone is long), so a dreamer reaches "seek knowledge" late.
  Whether the action subgraph's form served: as plain
  children ("It helps these.", "It depends on these choices.") yes; I
  did not use his relation nodes ("<a> helps <b>"), which would need a
  "helps" node made first and gave nothing a folder title does not.
  The one thing for Jeff, if any: in the fast-unique-title-links
  fork, his "love" is an alias, not a title, so a lookup by exact title
  would miss the example he wrote it for.
